  "summaries": [
    {
      "start": 0.0,
      "end": 4.0,
      "text": "I've added a counter to keep track of how many times the user types \"right\", with a twist: if they type it more than twice, they get to build a table and flip it. The lecture is now explaining that while loops are suitable for situations where you don't know how many times a user will input something."
    },
    {
      "start": 4.0,
      "end": 64.0,
      "text": "The loop counter is being incremented in a separate line to keep track of the number of iterations. Without this increment, the loop would be infinite because the condition would never change."
    },
    {
      "start": 64.0,
      "end": 124.0,
      "text": "The program prints the letter 'p' repeatedly if not stopped, slowing the computer, and hitting control C will terminate it. To avoid infinite loops when using counters or variables in while loops, variables must be initialised before use and incremented correctly."
    },
    {
      "start": 124.0,
      "end": 184.0,
      "text": "The lecturer is explaining a for loop, which can be used to replace four lines of code with just two, by printing a sequence of numbers from 0 to 4. The for loop variable n takes on the values 0 to 4, incrementing each time through the loop."
    },
    {
      "start": 184.0,
      "end": 244.0,
      "text": "The for loop variable increments to the next value in the sequence each time through the loop, starting with zero. The code block is executed with the variable's value changing on each iteration, after all expressions within the block are done."
    },
    {
      "start": 244.0,
      "end": 300.0,
      "text": "You can customise the range function to start from a specific value and/or increment by a certain step, giving you more flexibility than the default behaviour. To do this, you need to provide the stop value, and the start and step values will default to zero and one, respectively, if not specified."
    }
  ],
